WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.815 --> 00:00:13.190
LEIGHA JARETT: Hi, and welcome
to our session on building

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.850
an interactive machine learning
application using both Vertex

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.110
AI and Looker.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.000
I'm Leigha Jarett,
and I'm a developer

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.620
advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.330
And I'm also joined by my
colleague Sara Robinson.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.130
Before we get into
the details, let's

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.860
talk about why you might want
to build an interactive data

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.520
science application
for your team.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.430
Well, we know that business
users of all different types

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.550
need access to the results
of machine learning models.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.798
And we really want to
eliminate that bottleneck

00:00:42.798 --> 00:00:44.840
so that they don't need
to go through a data team

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.400
to get access to the results.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.520
We also want to make sure
that our data science

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.790
application has an
easy-to-use and simple UI

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.310
so that people of all
different technical levels

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.830
are able to interact with
machine learning models

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:01.260
and get the data that they
need to do their jobs.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.480
Finally, we want
to provide guidance

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.670
onto how business
users should interpret

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.460
the results of machine
learning and also take action

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.520
on them so we're making sure
that machine learning is

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.488
having a real influence on
business decision making.

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:17.030
Now in this
presentation, we're going

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.890
to talk about how
Google Cloud can

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.230
help you build these data
science applications.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.530
So let's pretend
that we are working

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.980
for a marketing department
of an e-commerce company.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.190
I might decide to create two
different models in Vertex AI.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.980
First, I'm going to create
a classification model

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.860
to predict whether or
not different users who

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.590
see an advertisement are
likely to make a purchase.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.930
Then we're going to create
a time series forecasting

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.410
model to predict our different
revenue so we can accurately

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.650
measure the lift
of those campaigns.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.200
Now, in order to train
our Vertex AI models,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.900
we're going to supply data
that's coming from our BigQuery

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.400
data warehouse.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.740
This gives us the
scale and reliability

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.440
that BigQuery offers
so we're able to store

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.820
and transform and prepare our
data for machine learning.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.150
Finally, we're going to use
Looker to actually create

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.460
a custom application
that our business

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.470
users can interact with.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.210
Using Looker's
Extension Framework,

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:19.420
we can customize the UI
exactly for our needs.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.080
And we'll enable our users
to interact with this UI

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.990
and have the results of
their interactions sent back

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:30.390
to Vertex AI to create new
predictions from our models.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.980
Now I'm going to
pass it off to Sara

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.440
to talk more about Vertex AI.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.690
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks, Leigha.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.200
Let's start by looking at what
Vertex AI is at a high level.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.050
Vertex AI is our end-to-end
managed machine learning

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.755
platform on Google Cloud.

00:02:44.755 --> 00:02:47.130
It's designed to help you with
every step of your machine

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.350
learning workflow, and
it's designed for people

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:50.910
regardless of ML expertise.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.040
So whether you have deep
experience building custom

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.470
models, or you're just getting
started with machine learning,

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.030
we have ways that
you can use Vertex

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.220
AI to add to your machine
learning applications.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.790
So here we can see an overview
of all the different products

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.190
that are included in
Vertex AI for each phase

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.140
of a typical machine
learning workflow.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.060
We're going to focus
just on a few of these

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.310
in this presentation,
starting with data sets,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.920
showing you how to
link data in BigQuery

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.870
to a data set in Vertex AI.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.150
Then we'll look at how
to train models in Vertex

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.835
and get predictions
on those models.

00:03:19.835 --> 00:03:21.210
And throughout
this presentation,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.120
we'll be focused on models
trained on tabular data

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.120
to build both a classification
and a forecasting model.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.970
Let's start by
looking at data, which

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.800
is usually the first part of
any machine learning workflow.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.700
So in Vertex AI, you start
by uploading a data set.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.980
And you do this by
selecting the type of data

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.530
you're working with, along
with the prediction task

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:43.488
that you'll be solving.

00:03:43.488 --> 00:03:44.863
In this presentation,
we're going

00:03:44.863 --> 00:03:46.850
to be working with
tabular data, but we also

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.630
support a lot of
other data types.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.770
And if your data doesn't fit
into any of these categories,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:52.520
don't worry.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.730
You can still use Vertex AI to
train your own custom models.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.530
Next let's look at
a decision framework

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.180
that I use to help
customers figure out

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.430
which parts of Vertex
AI are right for them.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.580
The first question to
ask is whether or not

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.820
you have your own training
data to use for your model.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.420
If the answer is no, we have
a set of pre-trained APIs

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.760
that give you access to
machine learning models

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.399
that have already been trained
on lots and lots of data

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:21.853
that you can access
via a REST API.

00:04:21.853 --> 00:04:23.270
For this presentation,
we're going

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.430
to be focused on the right
side of this flowchart.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.472
We're assuming that you
do have your own training

00:04:27.472 --> 00:04:30.080
data that you want to use to
train custom machine learning

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.080
models.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.630
The next question
to ask yourself

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.450
is whether or not you're going
to be writing the model code.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:36.950
And whatever the
answer is to this,

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.380
we have tools to support
you on Vertex AI.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:42.060
We're going to be focused
on AutoML for training,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.810
but you can also use Vertex
AI to train your own custom

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:49.310
models built with any
machine learning framework.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.040
Let's take a closer look
at AutoML for training.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.310
AutoML is a tool that lets you
train state of the art machine

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.590
learning models in less
time, because you're not

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.740
required to write any of that
model training code yourself.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.380
All you do is upload
your data to Vertex,

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:04.940
select the columns
that you're going

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.040
to be using for training,
along with the column that

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.665
has your label or the thing that
your model will be predicting.

00:05:09.665 --> 00:05:12.560
You press a Train
button, and Vertex AI

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.930
will iterate over different
model architectures

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.440
to find the best one suited
for your prediction task.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.090
When your model
is done training,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.790
you'll then get access to
detailed model evaluation

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.950
metrics along with
feature attributions.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.247
Once you have a
trained model, there

00:05:29.247 --> 00:05:31.330
are a couple of options
for how to get predictions

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.540
on that model.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.270
The first is you can
deploy your model

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:36.490
to an endpoint in Vertex AI.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.960
And endpoints live in
the same place in the UI

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.160
whether you train the model
using AutoML or your own custom

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:42.940
model code.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:46.330
You can also access both types
of models via the Vertex AI

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:47.380
SDK.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.290
You can split traffic
between different models,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:53.020
and you can also customize
the machine type where

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:54.115
your endpoint is deployed.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:58.800
And if latency is
less of a concern

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.570
and you have a lot
of test examples

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.070
that you want to
get predictions on,

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.110
you might want to create
a batch prediction job.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.240
And we'll show how to
do this in the demo.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.620
To create a batch
prediction, you

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.020
can do this either in
the Vertex AI console,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.210
or via the Vertex AI SDK.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.980
And you'll specify
where your data

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.200
is that you want to get
batch predictions on,

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.870
and then where you want
Vertex AI to write the results

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.400
of those batch predictions.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.460
In the demo, we'll be creating
a batch prediction job based

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.190
on data we have in BigQuery.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.938
And without further ado,
let's take a look at a demo.

00:06:29.938 --> 00:06:31.480
In this demo, I'm
going to be showing

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.580
how to train two different
models using data

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:34.570
from BigQuery.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:37.810
We'll train a classification
model, along with a time series

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.040
forecasting model.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.673
And we'll use AutoML to
train both of those models.

00:06:41.673 --> 00:06:43.090
When the models
are done training,

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:44.950
we'll look at some
evaluation metrics,

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.830
and then we'll see how we can
create batch prediction jobs so

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.110
that we can then visualize
the results of those model

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.090
predictions in our
Looker dashboard.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.600
Let's go to the demo.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.940
We'll train two models in
Vertex AI using our retail data.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.590
First, we need to import our
data from BigQuery into Vertex.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.160
There's a direct integration
between Vertex AI and BigQuery.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.140
Here we'll create a data set.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.020
This data set will be used to
train a tabular classification

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.190
model.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.610
We want to import data from
BigQuery, so we'll select here.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.050
And this is the
BigQuery table we

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.060
want to use to train our model.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.740
It's some data on
orders, and we'll

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:23.370
use it to predict whether or not
a customer will make an order.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.900
We'll enter that
BigQuery table here.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:29.052
And now we're ready to
train a model on this data.

00:07:29.052 --> 00:07:30.510
We'll train our
model using AutoML,

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.850
which means that we don't
need to worry about writing

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.380
any of the model code.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:36.210
Our target column is made_order.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:40.570
This will tell us whether or not
the customer made a purchase.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:43.950
Here we'll select the columns
we want to use for training.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.310
Here we want AutoML to maximize
the accuracy on our model

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.740
for the less common class.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.490
We're doing this because the
percentage of customers who

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.010
made an order in our data set
is much smaller than those who

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.430
didn't.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.320
And with that, we're
ready to start training.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.600
We'll follow the same process
to train a forecasting

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:07.380
model to predict sales for
different item categories.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:09.000
Here we're using
seven days of data

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.890
to predict what the
category sales will

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.910
be seven days in the future.

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.490
Now that we've
trained two models,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.410
let's see how each one
performed, starting

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:18.390
with our classification model.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.340
Here we can see that 77%
of the time our model

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.760
was able to correctly
predict when a customer would

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.230
make a purchase.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.910
We can also see
feature importance.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:31.910
This shows us which features
signaled our model's prediction

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:32.600
the most.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:35.360
In this case, it looks like
the advertisement category

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.820
was the most important
indicator of whether or not

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.190
someone would make a purchase.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.860
With our trained model, we can
now deploy it to an end point

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.930
to get online predictions.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:47.360
We can also request
batch predictions

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.210
by creating a batch prediction
job, either in the UI,

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:54.710
or programmatically
via the Vertex AI API.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:56.360
The result of our
batch production job

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:58.700
was written to a BigQuery table.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:01.280
For each row, we can see
the percentage likelihood

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:03.740
that a purchase was made.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.950
Here it looks like there is a
71% chance that the customer

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:09.290
made a purchase.

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:11.270
Let's also take a look
at how our forecasting

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:12.260
model performed.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.590
Here we can see some evaluation
metrics on our model.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:17.210
When we kicked off
our training job,

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.850
we specified that we'd like
test predictions written

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:21.180
to a BigQuery table.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:22.820
Let's take a look
at that table now.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:25.370
Here we can see the sales
predicted by our model

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.530
compared to the actual
sales on that date.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.530
Now let's see how to
create a batch prediction

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:33.740
job, looking at one window
of our time series data.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:35.810
When we're formatting
our data for prediction,

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.240
we'll pass it a BigQuery
table with sales

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.365
data for our seven-day
context window,

00:09:40.365 --> 00:09:41.990
and then we'll provide
the date we want

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:43.370
to generate a prediction on.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.070
For this model, it's
seven days in the future.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:48.500
We'll leave the sales
value for that row blank,

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.180
since that's what
we want to predict.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.180
We can get predictions on as
many seven-day time series

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:54.350
windows as we'd like.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:58.580
Here we're just showing
one for demo purposes.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:00.770
When this batch
prediction job completes,

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.200
we'll see the resulting
predicted sales value

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.840
for the date that we specified.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:07.400
In our Looker
dashboard, we'll be

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.500
able to visualize the
predictions resulting

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.573
from both of these models.

00:10:11.573 --> 00:10:12.740
LEIGHA JARETT: Thanks, Sara.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.470
So now we understand
how to actually create

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.530
models in Vertex AI and
even hit an end point

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:20.180
to get new predictions.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.350
But how do we extend
these capabilities

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.440
so that non-technical
business users

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.780
are able to interact
with Vertex AI

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.870
and analyze the results
of those predictions?

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:33.090
This is where Looker comes in.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.500
So you may already be familiar
with Looker's architecture.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.780
Looker enables you
to create a data

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:40.520
model, your single
source of truth

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.290
for all of your
different metrics.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:44.870
And it sits directly on
top of your data warehouse,

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:47.550
so BigQuery in our case.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.110
For the data
science application,

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:51.150
we might create a model
where we're actually

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.780
defining metrics associated with
the prediction results coming

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.670
from Vertex AI.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:11:00.180
This will allow our business
users to explore the results

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.760
and create visualizations
or dashboards,

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.310
and even operational
workflows to take action

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:07.480
on those results.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:09.060
But how do we
actually enable them

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.480
to interact with that endpoint
and create different inputs

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.070
that feed into Vertex AI?

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:18.520
This is where the Extension
Framework comes into play.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.170
Looker's Extension
Framework allows

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.690
you to create custom
JavaScript to control

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:25.840
the UI for a custom application
posted in the Looker

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:27.360
environment.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.770
So the great thing about the
Looker Extension Framework

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:34.180
is it handles all hosting,
authentication, authorization,

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.880
and gives you access
to Looker's APIs,

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:39.160
ability to embed
Looker components,

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:40.750
like dashboards and looks.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.650
And also, we have a set of
open source UI components

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:47.690
to really accelerate
your time to value.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:50.510
This diagram really helps
put it into perspective.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.610
If you want to go and
build a data science

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.440
application on
your own, you have

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:57.710
to take care of all of that
stuff outside of Looker.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.960
But with the Extension
Framework, so much of it

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:03.050
is taken care of for
you under the hood.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.010
So just to put these
two pieces together,

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:11.070
first, we're going to create a
dashboard using native Looker

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.000
technology.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.740
We'll model the
prediction results.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:15.690
We'll explore them,
and create a report

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:18.060
that business users can use.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:21.330
Next we'll use the Extension
Framework to actually customize

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.400
the UI so it's purpose
built for our models,

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:27.090
and so that users can
control the inputs that

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:31.910
are fed back into the
Vertex AI prediction job.

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:35.710
So let's actually see a demo
of all of this in action.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:37.930
I'll jump into my data
science application

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:42.130
so we can see how a marketing
person would interact with it.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:45.580
In Looker, I can navigate to
my data science application

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:49.480
from the left side panel under
the Applications dropdown,

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:51.850
or if you're using an
earlier version of Looker,

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:54.440
from the Browse dropdown.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.990
Now I can see my
custom-built application

00:12:56.990 --> 00:12:59.930
for the marketing team to
create targeted advertisement

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:01.550
campaigns.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:03.530
On the left-hand
side, marketers are

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:05.750
able to control
inputs to the model,

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.970
like selecting users
from a certain traffic

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:13.850
source, country, gender, age,
and previous purchase history.

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:16.550
They can also specify
what type of advertisement

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:17.630
they're planning to run.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.940
For example, here
we're predicting

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:23.480
if we fed an advertisement
campaign for accessories

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:26.450
through a search channel.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.610
On the right-hand side,
we have our dashboard

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:31.410
to actually analyze the
results of the model.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.650
So here we can see that
when we ran the model,

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:39.010
we fed in over 65,000 users
that had an average age of 53,

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.930
a certain average
prior spending habits,

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:44.450
and we can also see their
geographic location.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.180
Scrolling down, we
can start to analyze

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.810
the results of the batch
predictions from Vertex AI.

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:57.250
So here I can see that
2.6% of our user base

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:59.800
was expected to make a purchase.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.260
We can also use that
information to calculate

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:07.510
our predicted campaign cost
if we just targeted that 2.6%.

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:10.400
And we can slice our
likelihood to make a purchase,

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:13.510
so that predicted measure
coming from Vertex AI,

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:17.170
by other fields in
the user information,

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:19.120
like how many orders
they previously

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:21.170
made, what their
traffic source was,

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:24.380
and what their age group was.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.650
Next, we can look at the results
of our time series forecasting

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:28.350
model.

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:32.240
So to actually see what our
predicted revenue if we don't

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.670
do anything is for
the next seven days,

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:39.510
and see that compared to
prior historical sales.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.600
Now this dashboard is just
like any old Looker dashboard.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:46.530
So as a marketer, I can
explore from here, drill in.

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.560
So I might want to
explore and actually send

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:52.980
these users who we predict
to make a purchase off

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:55.560
to an external
application to target them

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:58.830
for that advertisement.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.380
Now that we understand how
a marketer would interact

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:03.660
with this application,
let's start

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:05.700
thinking about how
we actually went

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.100
and built this application.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:11.130
Like we said earlier, we built
this using Looker's Extension

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:12.360
Framework.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:16.620
So what we did was created some
lightweight custom JavaScript

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:21.030
to control the UI that
we're seeing here.

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.620
On the left-hand
side, I actually

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.840
used Looker's
components to create

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:26.830
each one of these inputs.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:31.420
Let's see what one of these
components might look like.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.030
For example, the
CheckboxGroup component

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.740
is used for our first input.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:39.100
Now, I'm a data person, and
not a front end developer.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:42.310
And although I did have to learn
a bit of JavaScript and React

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.690
to get everything
working appropriately,

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:46.510
most of the
application was built

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:48.940
leveraging these
components and working off

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:53.450
the Extension Framework code
templates that are available.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.510
Now if a marketer wants to
change the inputs to the model,

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:59.450
for example only focusing
on people in the US

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:01.040
and maybe seeing
what would happen

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:06.320
if we ran an advertisement
for jeans, I can click Run.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:08.900
And I'm going to have
to verify my identity

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:11.840
to make sure I have permission
to run the Vertex model.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.790
And then in the
Extension Framework,

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:17.180
my code is actually going to
send this information to Vertex

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:20.340
to create a batch
prediction job.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.820
Now before Looker
actually talks to Vertex,

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:27.440
it uses the Looker API to
create a BigQuery table that

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.940
contains one row for each person
in that audience we created

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:33.440
to predict on.

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.720
And you can see we're going
to have the jeans search

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:38.330
campaign, which is the
fields that the user entered

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.580
to actually see
what would happen

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:43.790
if we ran a certain
type of advertisement.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:46.400
And then the details of
the BigQuery table, we

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:48.410
can see that it's set
to expire tomorrow

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:51.800
so that we aren't storing too
much unnecessary information.

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:54.860
And also, it's named
with this long hash.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:59.120
And this is basically a unique
identifier for the prediction

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:01.360
that we're running.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:03.650
Now if we open up
our extension code,

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:06.160
we can see that after
the BigQuery table has

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:10.690
been created, it's time to make
our Vertex prediction call.

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:13.569
Here we're using
Looker's extension SDK

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:18.220
to create a POST request
to Google Cloud platform.

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:21.609
And in the input we're going to
specify things like our model

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:24.220
ID, the location where
this needs to run,

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:28.510
and also our input data, so that
BigQuery table we just created,

00:17:28.510 --> 00:17:30.700
and our output
BigQuery data set.

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:35.310
Since batch
predictions sometimes

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:37.962
take a few minutes to run,
we send the user a message

00:17:37.962 --> 00:17:39.420
on Google Chat when
the results are

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.190
ready with a link to review.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.830
Here we also include the
same hash used earlier

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.120
to bring the user back to
the results of the model

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:47.550
so that they can
use the dashboard

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:50.410
to investigate their results.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:53.770
The dashboard itself is just
a regular old Looker dashboard

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.840
that's embedded
into our application

00:17:55.840 --> 00:17:57.820
using Looker's embed SDK.

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.790
So just like previous
Looker API calls we made,

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:02.680
this requires just
a few lines of code

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:04.690
and no authentication needed.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:08.170
We use the embed methods to
pass in values for the filters

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:11.780
based on what the user
has entered on the left.

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:15.960
You'll also notice that we have
a parameter for our table name.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.280
This is the hash that was used
to identify the prediction,

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.680
and it's what's going
to be used to make sure

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:24.590
that we're leveraging
the right table that

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:27.770
stores the results of the latest
prediction results from Vertex

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:29.360
AI.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.490
Now the great thing
about this entire app

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:34.850
is that it was simple to build,
since Looker and Vertex do all

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:37.580
the hard parts, like model
and front end development,

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.860
deployment, and authentication.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:42.350
And my marketers can
interact with machine

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:45.690
learning all on their own to
get data when they need it.

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.520
Thanks again for
joining us today.

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:50.300
So we talked about how to
build a complete data science

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:53.240
application using
Vertex AI and Looker.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:56.330
Sara walked us through the
Vertex AI platform, including

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:59.120
how to create, train,
and deploy models,

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:01.700
and I showed you how to explore
the results of those models

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:05.120
in Looker and create your
own custom application using

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:07.160
Looker's Extension Framework.

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:10.220
So to get you well on your
way to creating your own data

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:13.340
science application, we
have listed some resources,

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:16.820
like Looker and Vertex AI
training courses and labs.

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:19.100
We also included some
other Next sessions

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:22.590
that might be relevant to
what we talked about today.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.770
So thanks again, Sara,
for joining us and talking

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:26.090
about Vertex AI.

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:28.590
And thank all of you
for joining us today.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:30.050
We hope you enjoyed
this session,

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:33.580
and that you enjoy
the rest of Next 2021.

